WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.615
A load balancer can improve reliability,

00:00:02.615 --> 00:00:05.720
but it can also hide problems until users feel them.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.970
Health checks should reflect real readiness: the process is alive, the API responds,

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:13.400
the needed model is available, and recent requests are not failing at an unhealthy rate.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.800
A server with an open port but constant generation timeouts is not a healthy target.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.840
Monitor both the front endpoint and every backend.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.610
At the gateway, track request volume, response times, status codes, timeouts,

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.785
streaming interruptions, and queues.

00:00:25.785 --> 00:00:29.675
On each backend, track resource pressure, active requests, model-loaded status,

00:00:29.675 --> 00:00:32.145
runtime errors, restarts, and failures.

00:00:32.145 --> 00:00:35.920
Without backend visibility, you may know the service is slow without knowing why.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.015
Many troubleshooting problems come from mismatches.

00:00:38.015 --> 00:00:41.620
A backend may run a different runtime version, expose a different endpoint,

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:45.400
use a different quantization, or map the same model name to a different file.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.400
A proxy may strip a required header.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.800
A timeout between layers may interrupt streaming.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.520
Load balancing increases the need to keep assumptions consistent.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.275
Failover and retries should match the workload.

00:00:55.275 --> 00:00:57.560
Retrying before a response starts may be safe.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.280
Retrying after streaming begins may confuse the client.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.600
Retrying an agent request that performed tool actions can duplicate work.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.120
Simple dashboards showing backend health, model availability, active requests, failures,

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.800
and queue growth are enough to tell whether load balancing is helping or hiding trouble.
